A||||6||Homicidal||Air still in the clutch? MORE bleeding??!? ||||1||
Z||000000||Homicidal||04-25-2000||08:24 PM||||I bled the clutch, but my pedal still feels light and not much spring or pressure to it. I can start the car in neutral with the pedal depressed but not in any gears. It just lurches foward some and then dies.<BR>Do I still have air in the system or is my problem more than just air?? <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Shiner||04-25-2000||10:50 PM||||It's really hard to say without actually operating the clutch in person.  Have somebody sit in the car and push the clutch while you check to see if the slave cylinder piston is moving far enough.  If it's not, then inspect all the pipes and tubes up to the master cylinder for leaks.  Check the master for leaks also.  They don't have to be very large.<P> Did you try gravity bleeding?  I am sure somebody else on the forum with more time will exlplain it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000002||Greg Piet||04-26-2000||01:29 AM||||My problem was a bad slave cylinder...   since it is so close to exhaust manifold y pipe it is a good guess...    get an assistant and check it out...   my slave didnt leak but drew air into the system||||reg||1
Z||000003||Homicidal||04-26-2000||03:04 AM||||How far should it move?  I think it moves 1/2-1 full inch.<P>Should it move more?<P>Gravity bleeding=opening bleescrew, filling resevoir and walking away for 15-30 minutes, right?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||clayell||04-26-2000||03:32 AM||||bleeding the clutch is the worst part of the job. the key to this is bleed, bleed, and bleed some more. when i replaced my slave cylinder i bled my clutch for hours thought i had fixed it and got down the road and lost my clutch again. i thought i had ruined my clutch, but there was still a tiny bit of air in the line. my car set for a week and a half. until finnally i tried again to bleed it and this time it stuck. so keep goin' it will come.||||reg||12
Z||000005||clayell||04-26-2000||03:49 AM||||there are two ways i was taught to bleed a clutch, the way i prefer is to take a small hose put it in a jar to catch the fluid. make sure your resevoir is full at all times.(very important). pump your clutch a few times. then push your clutch in and hold it down while someone else releses the valve(i had to do this myself). do this until you see no air in the hose. the other way is to fill the jar with fluid. make sure your hose reaches into the fluid at all times. open your valve and keep it open while pumping the clutch. the reason i dont like the second way is because it puts dirty fluid back into your system, so try the first way first.||||reg||1
Z||000006||SpeedPhreak||04-26-2000||05:40 PM||||dont know what year yours is but if its an 84 u probably need to change the clutch pedal.  bleed per v8 archie... i dont have the link but a quick search here will find it.  i found it important to bleed the slave cylinder & gravity bleed.<P>i jacked up the front end, kept the master full, opened the valve & then i did the slave per v8 archie (basically take the bleeder valve out & use a screw drive or sumthing to push the rod back into the slave cylinder.. hold the rod in while u replace the valve).<P>i know this is gonna ruffle some feathers... i used automatic transmission fluid instead of the hydrolic fluid.  now... some people here had problems w/this eventually & others did not.. i lost my car about 2 months after & it was still working fine.... some one else used jack oil & said it work great too... so alternative fluids may help u out as well.... just be warned that it can mess things up & u may end up rebuilding your hydrolics front to back.||||reg||1
